            <div xml:id="d924">

                <pb facs="0859" n="851" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg851"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/632: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-04-18T19:00:00-05:00">April 18, 1940—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>119. Tientsin’s 54, April 15, noon, and 55, April 17, 4 p.m.<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d924-f1"/> Please make an approach to the Japanese Foreign Office in
                    such manner as you may deem appropriate but without mention of the Tientsin
                    Chamber of Commerce and point out emphatically that the long-continued and
                    unlawful interference by Japanese armed forces and their agencies with the
                    movement of American citizens and of American-owned <pb facs="0860" n="852"
                        xml:id="pg852"/> merchandise at Tientsin has been the subject of repeated
                    conversations between American and Japanese officials but without cessation of
                    such interference. The Department suggests that you say that it seems obvious
                    that further instruction from Tokyo to the Japanese armed forces at Tientsin
                    will be required to effect an improvement in the situation and that you ask that
                    such instruction be sent.</p>
                <p>Sent to Tokyo via Peiping. Repeated to Chungking and Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
